WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.589
Film Courage: Saroo, from the beginning of
when you wrote your book [A Long Way Home:

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:09.179
A Memoir] until now, how long of a process
was this?

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.530
Saroo Brierley: 2012?

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.889
April I think?

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.380
So this is 2017, so four years?

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:25.240
Four and a half years.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.330
But it took me a year to write the book.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.520
So I was pretty fast.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.380
Film Courage: Okay.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.600
And then how did you present the book for
adaption for [screenwriter] Luke (Davies)

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.600
to write it?

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.870
Or how did that whole process turn out.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:44.480
Saroo Brierley: The process was Luke read
the book and then I said to Cecil Films and

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:53.620
then I said I’ve got to get Luke to India
and take him through the journey that I went

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:59.309
through and I thought that would give him
another dimension of feel and atmosphere that

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:08.970
you would sort of get from reading a book
so and I wanted to take him back from right

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:09.970
where it started.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:15.690
Do the train journey and all the places that
I went to.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.340
And then come to Australia and see if it rings
true as well.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.220
Because I think if I didn’t do that, it
wouldn't have had clarity.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:34.710
It wouldn't be sort of as clean and it would
really sort of help him in terms of writing

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.710
the script.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.070
Film Courage: For me LION has been my favorite
narrative film for the year and I’m not

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.750
just saying this because you’re in front
of me here.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.110
I’m really excited to tell others about
it and I have.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.060
The whole package is beautiful with the music
and everything.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.860
For you, I’m wondering what is an inspiring
film that has touched your life as LION has

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.860
touched mine.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.010
It can be from any year.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.890
It does’t have to be this one [2016-17]?

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.920
Saroo Brierley: It’s very relevant actually
to LION and it’s this other movie.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.340
It’s called the JOY LUCK CLUB.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:10.289
I don’t know if you’ve heard of it?

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:19.496
Particularly, where the mother and the children
meet in the room there…..and at the end

00:02:19.496 --> 00:02:22.940
of the movie, I meet my mother and it’s
very touching to me.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.240
I have a very soft spot for that movie.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.630
But it’s an amazing movie that I saw at
a young age and remember it still to this

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:32.630
day.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:37.330
Film Courage: So when you saw that film and
you began working with Luke Davies on the

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.810
screenplay [for LION], did you want people
to come away with the same feeling, or a similar

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.670
feeling, or really because it was about your
life you were not able to remove yourself?

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.900
Since that movie impacted you, did you want
others to be impacted, as well?

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.950
Film Courage: Well of course I would have.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:03:02.349
That was up to Luke to make that variation
of that certain situation with Garth to make

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:12.590
it come alive so I guess that’s such a pivotal
point in the movie as well that particular

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.849
bit, reuniting together mother and son.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:23.920
I think that the way Luke has done it is really,
really amazing and really wanted to get down

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.859
to the grass roots and more particularly that
sort of scene because apparently that was

00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:31.950
the first scene shot and I couldn’t believe
it when he told me.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:43.379
But a lot of people were particularly enthralled
with this scene because it would resonate

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:54.379
with a lot of people especially mothers and
people who have lost their mothers as well.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:58.569
But I think that it touches me overtime that
I see it.

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:07.129
Film Courage: What book for you has been instrumental
in the creative process, in writing for you.

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:10.579
What book [can you recommend] on writing,
creativity?

00:04:10.579 --> 00:04:16.639
Saroo Brierley: That’s a hard one because
I’ve only been really reading magazines

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:26.120
and comics and sometimes reading Mum’s beauty
magazines, Cosmopolitan and a few others but

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:32.729
I’m a first time author and I’ve never
written a book.

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:38.449
And I went from being into industrial retail
sales to just landing in this…to just doing

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:47.210
this journey but all the sudden becoming an
author and having been asked to write my memoir.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:53.400
Film Courage: And I understand you are working
with your Mom on a second book.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.400
Is that right?

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.400
Saroo Brierley: How did you know?

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.949
Film Courage: I heard it in another video…A
little birdie told me.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:58.310
Saroo Brierley: Yeah…we are.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.740
We’re about to start the prequel to LION
or A LONG WAY HOME.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:11.199
Which doesn't really have to do so much with
me but coming to the point just prior to me

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:17.270
which has mostly to do with mothers, my adopted
mother and my biological.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:25.409
And also one more lady which is Saroj Sood
who owns the orphanage and how those women

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:26.789
are all interrelated.

00:05:26.789 --> 00:05:34.610
And all for the one cause, the humanitarian
side to help others that are more in need.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:40.060
And I’m just getting goose bumps just talking
about it.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:49.810
It is so unique to have women from oceans
apart from different backgrounds all wanting

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:56.539
to do one thing and that is to help a child
and to give him or her another chance of life.

00:05:56.539 --> 00:05:59.889
Saroo Brierley: Well I could stay here all
night and talk with you but I know you have

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:00.889
to go.

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:08.939
But last question and that is for people of
an adult age who have a childhood that either

00:06:08.939 --> 00:06:13.789
they don’t remember or there are lots of
unanswered questions and they’re adults.

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:18.879
What would you recommend to them about either
going home or trying to find out more about

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:22.110
their own history so that they know who they
are, [and have] a sense of themselves?

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.890
Saroo Brierley: I’m not too sure but for
me it was finding closure.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.610
And for them I think it was about listening
to your heart and listening to your dreams

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.610
really.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:28.610
Because that is what it was for me.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:29.610
In a nutshell, encapsulated.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:30.610
And if they are longing for something that
has been lost for such a long time and it’s

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:31.610
such a weight on their shoulders then they
ought to pursue it.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:32.960
Because life is a little bit difficult when
you don’t know your identity and you don’t

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:39.160
know your past because…but give it a valiant
attempt and at least try to get answers and

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.820
closure to something that you can’t get
answers to because if you don’t try then

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:43.820
you don’t know.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:07:11.169
Film Courage: Well thank you and good luck
tonight, Saroo!

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:11.810
Saroo Brierley: Thank you!

